      "side": 1,
      "message": "answer from jetski:\n\n\n  No, only getter/query commands need genlmsg_reply(). Setter/action commands must not call genlmsg_reply().\n\n  Here is how Generic Netlink works:\n\n  ### 1. Getter / Query Commands (GET_INFO, GET_RAM_SIZE, READ)\n\n  • Purpose: Return payload data (attributes like APF_ATTR_INFO_*, APF_ATTR_RAM_SIZE, APF_ATTR_DATA) to userspace.\n  • Mechanism: The handler constructs a response message with apf_genlmsg_new(), populates the attributes with nla_put_*(), and delivers it back to userspace via genlmsg_reply().\n  ──────\n  ### 2. Setter / Action Commands (SET_ID, ENABLE, DISABLE, WRITE, SET_FAST_PATH)\n\n  • Purpose: Perform an operation or update hardware/driver state (no payload attributes returned).\n  • Mechanism: The handler only returns 0 on success or -errno on failure.\n  • Why no genlmsg_reply?:\n      • Netlink clients send mutation requests with the NLM_F_ACK flag in nlmsghdr.nlmsg_flags.\n      • The core Netlink framework in af_netlink.c:2556-2559 automatically replies with a standard netlink_ack() (NLMSG_ERROR packet with error \u003d 0 on success, or error \u003d -errno + extack on failure):\n\tack:\n\t    if (nlh-\u003enlmsg_flags \u0026 NLM_F_ACK || err)\n\t        netlink_ack(skb, nlh, err, \u0026extack);\n\n      • If a setter command were to manually call genlmsg_reply(), userspace would receive two responses (a blank custom message followed by the kernel\u0027s automatic ACK), breaking standard Netlink client libraries (libnl, libmnl).",
      "parentUuid": "0a78a078_fb930058",
